<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
{#- The <title> budget, #137. `metaTitle` fits the brand suffix to what is
    left of ~60 characters rather than always spending 37 of them; `seoTitle`
    lets a page whose own headline is longer than the budget give search a
    shorter one without touching the <h1>. See .eleventy.js for the reasoning
    and the measurements. -#}
<title>{{ (seoTitle or title) | metaTitle(site.name, site.tagline) }}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ description or site.description }}">
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}">
{% if noindex %}<meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow">{% endif %}

{#- Search Console's HTML-tag fallback (#135). Empty in the repo and expected
    to stay that way: the property is verified by a DNS TXT record at the
    registrar, which needs no deploy and survives a hosting change. This exists
    so that picking the other method is a one-token edit in _data/site.json
    rather than a template change made in a hurry. -#}
{% if site.googleSiteVerification %}<meta name="google-site-verification" content="{{ site.googleSiteVerification }}">{% endif %}

<!-- A link to this site is how the funnel starts, so the card it unfurls into
     is part of the product. summary_large_image was declared here for days
     with no image behind it, which renders as a blank card everywhere. -->
<meta property="og:type" content="{{ ogType or 'website' }}">
<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ site.name }}">
<meta property="og:title" content="{% if title %}{{ title }} — {% endif %}{{ site.name }}">
<meta property="og:description" content="{{ description or site.description }}">
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}">
<meta property="og:image" content="{{ site.url }}{{ ogImage or '/assets/img/og.png' }}">
<meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
<meta property="og:image:height" content="630">
{% if ogType == "article" %}<meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ title }} — Prospektor">
{% else %}<meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ ogAlt or 'Prospektor — your AI pre-sales team. $999 per month, per workspace.' }}">{% endif %}
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
<meta name="twitter:title" content="{% if title %}{{ title }} — {% endif %}{{ site.name }}">
<meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ description or site.description }}">
<meta name="twitter:image" content="{{ site.url }}{{ ogImage or '/assets/img/og.png' }}">

<link rel="icon" href="/assets/img/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/assets/img/favicon.svg">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#1A1A18">
<link rel="preload" href="/assets/fonts/plus-jakarta-sans-latin.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
<link rel="preload" href="/assets/fonts/jetbrains-mono-latin.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/css/fonts.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/css/main.css">

<!-- Cookie consent (#143), copied from the studio's #131. In the head and on
     every page, because the gate has to be in place before anything optional
     could run — Netlify's Real User Metrics tag is injected at the very end of
     <body>, and netlify/edge-functions/rum-consent.js hands it to this script
     rather than to the browser. Deferred, dependency-free, first-party. -->
<script src="/assets/js/consent.js" defer></script>

{#- Sitewide structured data (#137). The audit found none of this: eight of the
    seventeen indexable pages carried no structured data at all, and the only
    types on the site were `Article` (nine posts) and `FAQPage` (/help/). That
    leaves Google to infer from prose who publishes this site, what the product
    is and what it costs.

    `Organization` and `WebSite` go on EVERY page rather than only the
    homepage, and both carry an explicit `@id` — so the `Article` blocks under
    /resources/ and the `Product` block on /pricing/ can point a `publisher` or
    a `brand` at the same node instead of each restating it. That is what makes
    it one graph rather than a pile of unrelated blocks.

    Deliberately NOT here: `SoftwareApplication`. It wants an `applicationCategory`
    and an operating system, and it invites the app-install rich result — the
    wrong shape for a service sold by subscription. /pricing/ carries `Product`
    with a real `Offer` instead, which is the eligible one and the one with the
    price in it. -#}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#organization",
      "name": "{{ site.name }}",
      "url": "{{ site.url }}",
      "description": {{ site.tagline | dump | safe }},
      "email": "{{ site.email }}",
      "logo": {
        "@type": "ImageObject",
        "url": "{{ site.url }}/assets/img/favicon.svg"
      },
      "image": "{{ site.url }}/assets/img/og.png"
    },
    {
      "@type": "WebSite",
      "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#website",
      "url": "{{ site.url }}",
      "name": "{{ site.name }}",
      "description": {{ site.description | dump | safe }},
      "publisher": { "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#organization" },
      "inLanguage": "en"
    }
  ]
}
</script>
</head>
<body>

<nav>
  <a href="/" class="logo">
    <div class="logo-mark">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none"><path d="M2 6C2 3.79 3.79 2 6 2s4 1.79 4 4-1.79 4-4 4" stroke="white" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/><circle cx="6" cy="6" r="1.2" fill="white"/></svg>
    </div>
    {{ site.name }}
  </a>
  <ul class="nav-links" id="navLinks">
    {% for item in site.nav %}
    <li><a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.label }}</a></li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <div class="nav-right">
    <span class="nav-ai-badge">AI_FIRST</span>
    <a href="https://studio.prospektor.ai" class="nav-signin">Sign in</a>
    <a href="/#scan" class="nav-cta">Scan your site</a>
    {#- The phone's way in (#153). Until the nav became real pages, hiding it
        under 860px cost a visitor three anchors onto the page they were
        already reading — nothing. It now costs them /who-to-pitch/,
        /what-to-send/ and /pricing/, so the header needs a door on a phone.
        A <button> with aria-expanded rather than a checkbox hack, so it is
        announced as what it is; the panel is the same <ul>, re-laid-out by
        CSS, so there is exactly one nav in the markup and the tests that
        derive from site.nav keep covering both. No JS, no panel. -#}
    <button class="nav-toggle" id="navToggle" type="button"
            aria-controls="navLinks" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Menu">
      <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M3 6h18"></path><path d="M3 12h18"></path><path d="M3 18h18"></path></svg>
      <svg class="nav-toggle-x" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><path d="M5 5l14 14"></path><path d="M19 5L5 19"></path></svg>
    </button>
  </div>
</nav>

{{ content | safe }}

<footer>
  <a href="/" class="footer-logo">
    <div class="logo-mark">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 12 12" fill="none"><path d="M2 6C2 3.79 3.79 2 6 2s4 1.79 4 4-1.79 4-4 4" stroke="white" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round"/><circle cx="6" cy="6" r="1.2" fill="white"/></svg>
    </div>
    {{ site.name }}
  </a>
  <span class="footer-tagline">{{ site.tagline }}</span>
  <ul class="footer-links">
    {% for item in site.legal %}
    <li><a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.label }}</a></li>
    {% endfor %}
    {#- Not in site.legal, because it is not a page: consent.js catches the
        click by delegation and opens the preferences panel. Withdrawal has to
        be as easy as granting (GDPR Art. 7(3)), which means it has to be here,
        on every page, and not only on the visit where the banner appeared. -#}
    <li><a href="#cookies" data-cookies>Cookies</a></li>
  </ul>
  <span>{{ site.email }}</span>
</footer>

{#- defer (#137). This sat mid-document without it, so the parser stopped
    here and rendered nothing below until the script had been fetched and
    run. Every one of these is an IIFE that looks its elements up by id and
    returns if they are absent, so deferring is not only cheaper — it is the
    case the guard was written for, with the DOM guaranteed complete. -#}
<script src="/assets/js/main.js" defer></script>
</body>
</html>
